{"question": "A 26-year-old male presents to his primary care physician with complaints of burning with urination, penile discharge, and intermittent fevers. A urethral smear shows gram negative diplococci within white blood cells. The organism grows well when cultured on Thayer-Martin agar. The patient is prescribed a course of ceftriaxone and the infection resolves without further complication. One year later, the patient returns with the same infection. Which of the following best explains this lack of lasting immunity?", "options": {"A": "Exotoxin release", "B": "Antigenic variation", "C": "Polysaccharide capsule", "D": "Bruton's agammaglobulinemia", "E": "Lack of necessary vaccination"}, "pred_answer": "B", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Urology", "Infectious Disease", "Dermatology", "Immunology", "Sexually Transmitted Diseases"], "option_domains": ["Infectious Disease", "Immunology"], "question_analyses": {"Urology": "The medical scenario describes a 26-year-old male with recurrent urethritis, specifically caused by Neisseria gonorrhoeae, a gram-negative diplococcus. The patient's symptoms, including burning with urination, penile discharge, and intermittent fevers, are consistent with a gonococcal infection. The diagnosis is confirmed by the presence of gram-negative diplococci within white blood cells in the urethral smear and the growth of the organism on Thayer-Martin agar, a selective medium used for the isolation of Neisseria species. The patient is treated with ceftriaxone, a broad-spectrum antibiotic effective against N. gonorrhoeae, and the infection resolves. However, one year later, the patient returns with the same symptoms, indicating a recurrence of the infection. This scenario raises several important points:\n\n1. **Recurrent Infection**: The most alarming aspect is the recurrence of the infection despite appropriate treatment. Recurrent gonococcal urethritis can be due to reinfection from a sexual partner or persistent infection tha